{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Intervals","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.levelSum_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.levelSum_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":302,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L302-L317","doc":"**`Σ_λ dim_λ · y_λ = 1` at every `n`** — the normalisation `tr(1) = 1` for\nthe two-row weights, at general `n`.\n\nThis is what the file was for. Until now the identity existed only as the two\nexplicit instances `qdim_normalisation_three` and `qdim_normalisation_four`, with\n`n = 2 … 8` checked numerically in sympy; the general statement was asserted from\nthose. It is now proved, for every `n`, from `yw_branching` and Pascal.\n\nThe hypotheses are the standing non-degeneracy of the q-integers: `q ≠ 0`,\n`q ≠ q⁻¹` (else `[m]_q` is `0/0`), and `q ≠ −q⁻¹` (else `[2]_q = 0` and the\ntower divides by zero). At the substrate anchor `q₀ ≈ 1.11` all three hold. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.levelSum_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.levelSum_zero","kind":"theorem","line":298,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L298-L300","doc":"**The bottom of the tower.** One shape, dimension `1`, weight `[1]_q/[2]_q⁰`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.levelSum_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.levelSum_succ","kind":"theorem","line":253,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L253-L296","doc":"**The level sum does not depend on the level.**\n\nThis is `level_sum_step` at `N = n + 1`, with the ballot numbers for the\ndimensions and `yw` for the weights: Pascal supplies `hd`, `yw_branching`\nsupplies `hg`, and `yw_zero` supplies `hbdry`.\n\n`hg` splits into exactly three cases, and only the first is mathematics:\n\n* `2k ≤ n` — the branching identity itself, at `m = n − 2k`;\n* `2k = n + 1` — the dimension is zero, so the (false) identity is never used;\n* `2k ≥ n + 2` — every argument truncates to `0`, so all three weights vanish\n  and the identity reads `0 = 0 + 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.levelSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.levelSum","kind":"def","line":237,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L237-L251","doc":"**The level sum** `Σ_λ dim_λ · y_λ` over the two-row shapes of `n`.\n\nSummed over the whole of `range (n + 1)` rather than over `k ≤ ⌊n/2⌋`, because\nevery off-shape term vanishes on its own — and this is what removes the parity\ncase-split the argument otherwise needs:\n\n* at `2k = n + 1` the *dimension* is zero (`ballot_eq_zero_of_two_mul_eq_succ`);\n* at `2k ≥ n + 2` the ℕ subtraction truncates the weight's argument to `0`, and\n  the *weight* is zero (`yw_zero`).\n\nTwo different reasons, one uniform range. Taking instead the tight upper limit\n`⌊(n+1)/2⌋` forces the two levels' shape counts apart whenever `n` is odd and\nmakes the boundary hypothesis hold for a different reason in each parity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.ballot_eq_zero_of_two_mul_eq_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.ballot_eq_zero_of_two_mul_eq_succ","kind":"theorem","line":219,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L219-L235","doc":"**The ballot number vanishes one past the end of the row**: `d(n,k) = 0`\nwhenever `2k = n + 1`.\n\nFor `n = 2j+1` the shape `(n−k, k)` with `k = j+1` would need `n ≥ 2k = n+1`, so\nit does not exist, and the ballot number knows it: `C(n, j+1) = C(n, j)` by the\nsymmetry `n − (j+1) = j`, so the difference is zero.\n\nThis is the fact that rescues `hg` at the one index where the bare branching\nidentity fails — see `level_sum_step`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.qint_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.qint_one","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L214-L217","doc":"**`[1]_q = 1`.** The hypothesis cannot be dropped: `[1]_q` is literally\n`(q − q⁻¹)/(q − q⁻¹)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.ballot_pascal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.ballot_pascal","kind":"theorem","line":199,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L199-L212","doc":"**Pascal for the ballot numbers**, `d(n+1,k+1) = d(n,k+1) + d(n,k)`.\n\nTwo applications of Pascal's rule for binomials, and the `k = 0` case works out\nbecause the two `−C(n,0)` terms cancel against the `+1` of `ballot n 0`. This\nis the dimension half of `level_sum_step`'s `hd`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.ballot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.ballot","kind":"def","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L189-L197","doc":"The ballot / Catalan-triangle number `d(n,k) = dim` of the two-row irrep\n`(n−k, k)`, carried in `R`.\n\nDefined by cases on `k` rather than as `C(n,k) − C(n,k−1)` with truncating\nsubtraction: at `k = 0` that expression would read `C(n,0) − C(n,0) = 0`,\nbecause `0 - 1 = 0` in `ℕ`, where the true dimension is `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.yw_one_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.yw_one_succ","kind":"theorem","line":155,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L155-L187","doc":"**`yw q n 1 = yw q (n+1) 2`** — one level up, two steps along, same weight.\n\n`[1]_q = 1` and `[2]_q` is exactly the factor each level of the tower divides\nby, so the two cancel.\n\nThis is not decoration. In the `k`-indexing the branching hypothesis of\n`level_sum_step` is `yw q n (n − 2k + 1) = yw q (n+1) (n − 2k + 2) + yw q (n+1) (n − 2k)`,\nwhich is `yw_branching` at `m = n − 2k` **while `2k ≤ n`**. At the top index of\nan odd level, `2k = n + 1` and the ℕ subtraction truncates `n − 2k` to `0`, so\nthe required identity degenerates to `yw q n 1 = yw q (n+1) 2 + yw q (n+1) 0`.\nBy `yw_zero` the second term drops, and this lemma says what is left is true —\nso the hypothesis survives the truncation rather than being violated by it.\n\nThat is luck rather than design, and it is worth having as a named fact instead\nof a coincidence rediscovered inside a proof. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.yw_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.yw_zero","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L150-L153","doc":"**`yw q n 0 = 0`.** The shape \"one past the end of the row\" carries no\nweight, which is what `hbdry` asserts when `n` is even. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.qint_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.qint_zero","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L145-L148","doc":"**`[0]_q = 0`**, hence the weight at argument `0` vanishes. This is what\nkills the boundary term in the even case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.level_sum_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation.level_sum_step","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowNormalisation.lean#L73-L131","doc":"**The level sum is unchanged by one step of branching.**\n\n`d'` is the dimension family one level up, obtained from `d` by Pascal; `f` is\nthe weight family one level up and `g` the one at the current level, related by\nthe branching `g k = f k + f (k+1)`.\n\nThe two sums are then equal. Read right to left: every shape at the lower level\ndistributes its dimension over the two shapes above it, and each upper shape\ncollects contributions from the (at most two) shapes below it — so the total is\ncarried up unchanged.\n\n`hbdry` says the last dimension meets no shape above it. Without it the claim is\nfalse, not merely unprovable: the reindexed sum exceeds the target by exactly\n`d N * f (N + 1)`.\n\n**`hd` and `hg` are range-local, and must be.** They were universally\nquantified in the first version, which is unusable at the intended instance.\nThe weight family is `f k = yw q (n+1) (n − 2k + 2)`, and `hg` is exactly\n`yw_branching` at `m = n − 2k` — but only while `n − 2k` is the intended\ninteger. Past `k = n/2` the **ℕ** subtraction `n − 2k` truncates to `0`, so the\nuniversally-quantified hypothesis would assert\n`yw (n+1) 2 + yw (n+1) 0 = yw n 1` for every large `k`, which is a different\n(and false) claim about q-integers rather than a vacuous one. Off-shape indices\nhave to be excluded by the range, not by hoping the arithmetic degenerates\nharmlessly.\n\n**`hg` is stated weighted, `d k * g k = d k * (f k + f (k+1))`, not as the bare\n`g k = f k + f (k+1)`.** That is not stylistic. At the intended instance the bare\nform is *false* at one index: when `2k = n+1` the branching identity degenerates\nunder ℕ truncation to `yw q n 0 = yw q (n+1) 1 + yw q (n+1) 0`, i.e. `0 = [1]_q /\n[2]_q^{n+1}`, which is wrong rather than vacuous. What saves it is that the\ndimension there is `0` — the shape `(n−k, k)` with `2k > n` does not exist — and\nthe lemma only ever uses the product. Weighting the hypothesis is exactly the\ngenerality the proof already had. ","signatureChunk":0}]}